\F3 LESSON 2.1 -- INSERT MODE\F7

  i   insert before the cursor        a  insert after the cursor
  I   insert at first non-blank      A  insert at end of line
  o   open a line below              O  open a line above
  R   enter replace mode: typed keys overwrite, like overtyping

1. Move to the ---> line, cursor on the first flower, and press i, then
   type "tulip " and press Esc. Repeat for roses, daisies, whatever.

--->      flowers are nice.

2. Backspace at the start of a line joins it to the line above,
   precisely undoing Enter: cursor on the ---> line, press o (a new line
   opens below), then Backspace, then Esc.

---> press o, then Backspace, then Esc. As if nothing happened.

3. Enter inside insert mode splits the line at the cursor; Backspace at
   the start of the new line rejoins it with nothing lost.

---> this line will split here and rejoin.

4. R replaces as you type. Move to the ---> line, press R, mangle the word,
   then Esc. Notice -- REPLACE -- on the status line.

---> overwrite me (gently)

------------------------------------------------------------------------
\F3 LESSON 2.2 -- UNDO, JOIN, REPEAT\F7

  u   undo the last change. vi keeps ONE level of undo: pressing u again
      toggles the change back. This is not a bug; it is 1976.
  U   undo all changes to the line that changed last
  J   join the next line onto this one with one space
  .   repeat the last change that moved text

1. Move to the ---> lines and join them with J. Then u, then J again.

---> these two lines
---> yearn to be one.

1. Move to the ---> line, put the cursor on the first word, press dw,
   then press . again and again.

---> chop chop chop chop chop chop chop chop

3. . repeats insertions too: cw fresh text Esc, move to another word, and
   press .

---> change this word and this word and this word.

