\F3 LESSON 3.1 -- THE COMMAND LINE, PART ONE: GOING PLACES\F7

Press : to enter command-line mode. The status line becomes a prompt. Esc
cancels; Enter executes; up/down arrows walk the history.

  :5        go to line five              :$   go to the last line
  :+2       down two lines               :-1  up one line

1. Try :$ (end of file), then :1 (back), then :0 (very top; this tutorial
   has no line zero, so vi settles for line one).

------------------------------------------------------------------------
\F3 LESSON 3.2 -- THE COMMAND LINE, PART TWO: SED WITHOUT THE S \F7

  :s/old/new        replace the first "old" on the current line
  :s/old/new/       trailing slash: same thing, vi will not judge
  :%s/old/new/g     every line; the g replaces every match in a line

1. Move to the ---> line and run :s/fox/gnu/; then :%s/fox/gnu/g and
   watch both lines change at once.

---> the quick brown fox. the fox is quick.

2. Move to the ---> line and run :s/teh/the/, then :%s/lots/loads/g.

---> teh best of teh web has lots of typo and lots of charm.

3. :d deletes the current line (or N lines), :y yanks it, :put pastes the
   unnamed register below the cursor line. Like dd and p, but with more
   punctuation.

4. Save and quit, vi style:

  :w    "Saved. (To where? There's no disk. Trust me.)"
  :q    "Nowhere to go. You're here forever." (with unsaved changes, this
         is also the refusal, as in real vi)
  :q!   also refused, with feeling
  :wq   "Saved and quit. Wait-- you can't quit. You're still here."

Unknown commands answer "E492: Not an editor command", as they have since
time immemorial.

