\F3 LESSON 5.2 -- SENTENCES, PARAGRAPHS, AND MATCHING\F7

  (   previous sentence       )  next sentence
  {   previous paragraph      }  next paragraph
  %   match the bracket under or after the cursor: () [] {} nest

A sentence ends with ., ! or ? followed by a space; a paragraph ends at a
blank line.

1. Put the cursor on the first line of this paragraph and press } twice.
   The blank lines around lessons are paragraphs too.

2. Move to the ---> line and press % a few times.

---> (never [do "this" {sort of} thing] much)

------------------------------------------------------------------------
\F3 LESSON 6.1 -- OPERATORS, SYSTEMATICALLY\F7

The real grammar of vi: [count] operator [count] motion. The counts
multiply: 2d3w deletes six words. Operators so far: d c < > y and the case
motions:

  gu{motion}  lowercase the range      gU{motion}  uppercase it
  g~{motion}  toggle its case
  guu gUU g~~  like their doubled cousins dd yy, for the whole line

1. On the ---> line, use gU$ then undo, then gu$ and undo.

---> make me shout. or whisper. TOGGLE ME.

2. c works like d but enters insert mode: cw changes a word, cc a line,
   C to the end of the line. The classic is cw, which vi smartly keeps to
   the word itself, without eating the space after it.

3. Yank with y: yy yanks a line, yw a word. Yanking changes nothing --
   registers only. On the ---> lines, yy the first, p below the second.

---> yank me.
---> paste below me.

4. < and > shift lines left and right by one shiftwidth (a tab). >>, <<,
   and their counted forms all work.

---> shift me right. (Then left. Then complain to :q!)

------------------------------------------------------------------------
\F3 LESSON 7.1 -- THE MOUSE (ANACHRONISMS WELCOME)\F7

The original vi had no mouse. Ours humours you:

  * Click to move the cursor.
  * Press and drag to highlight a range. The highlight is only a
    highlight: on release it is gone, and a following d acts on the cursor
    position, as in real vi. Selection is the mouse's alone; vi has no v
    key for it. That arrived with vim, which is another story.
  * The wheel scrolls, moving the cursor line by line, like j and k.

Clicking works on every lesson line above. Go on; nothing is fragile here.

